# this entry produces carry the SAME plain "datadog_" public prefix v1's
# own resources carry, not a version-revealing "datadog_v2_" one. Before
# that field existed, the table's own key was the only input to wire
# naming -- see that PR's own description for the full real account.
#
# Real collision count, confirmed against this entry's own actual
# wire_name="datadog" output (not the earlier, naive-prefix estimate
# from before wire_name existed, which counted 3): datadog_event_response
# does NOT collide -- v2's own Phase 2 same-provider tie-break (a
# genuinely different v2 endpoint sharing event_response's response
# schema) bumps ITS OWN losing candidate to a self-disambiguated
# "datadog_v2_event_response" before this entry's output ever reaches
# the group merge below, leaving v1's plain datadog_event_response
# untouched and v2's own event-response variant surviving under a
# distinct name -- both real, neither excluded. Only 2 of v2's 148
# resources actually collide by generated wire name with v1's own
# richer version: datadog_application_key_response (v1: 5 fields, v2: 3)
# and datadog_user_response (v1: 9 fields, v2: 3) -- v1 wins both,
